#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Mobile-Robot Map Consistency Analysis -- robust SE(2) pose-graph optimization
==============================================================================
Inputs : data/poses_initial.npy, data/odometry_edges.csv,
         data/loop_candidates.csv, data/task_info.json
Outputs: poses.npy [544,3], loop_closure_scores.csv, metrics.json,
         map_diagnostics.png

Conventions (confirmed with researcher):
  z_ij = T_i^{-1} (x) T_j  (relative measurement in the local frame of pose i)
  e_ij = [ R(th_i)^T (p_j - p_i) - z_t ] / sigma_t   (2 translational rows)
         [ wrap(th_j - th_i - z_r) ]     / sigma_r   (1 rotational row)
  angles wrapped to +-pi; units m / rad.
  Omega = diag(1/st^2, 1/st^2, 1/sr^2) from the CSV sigma columns.
  Gauge: pose 0 hard-pinned to its initial value (world-frame anchor);
  its 3 dofs are eliminated from the normal equations.

Method:
  * sparse Gauss-Newton with backtracking on the full pose graph
  * GNC: graduated non-convexity on the LOOP edges with Cauchy weights
    w = k^2/(k^2+d^2), bandwidth k annealed from k0 down to kmin=1
    (dimensionless residuals). Odometry is a LINEAR (unrobustified)
    backbone: a Cauchy kernel on odometry was tested and REJECTED - it
    admits spurious minima that bend the odometry chain by meters to
    please false-loop clusters (see metrics.model_selection).
  * 61 initialization / schedule variants (4 forward schedules, 2
    stratified seeds, 55 single-loop seeds) compared by a common robust
    objective; best kept
  * hard-trim re-optimization (linear odometry + Cauchy k=1 loops) until
    the inlier set is stable
  * scoring from OPTIMIZED residuals only (raw pre-optimization residuals
    are all >5 sigma and are NEVER used for scoring):
      false_probability   = sigmoid((d - 2.5)/0.4)       [primary]
      false_prob_chi2_alt = sigmoid((d^2 - 11.345)/6.0)  [chi2_3 99pct point]
    cross-checked with per-variant IRLS outlier-hit frequency.

No matplotlib in this environment (no network): map_diagnostics.png is
rendered by a pure-numpy grayscale rasterizer + zlib PNG writer using a
3x5-stroke ASCII vector font.
"""
